  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1114",
      "html": "<p>Rita chrysea Day, 1877. Common name: Mahanadi Rita. Size: 19.5 cm SL. Type locality: Orissa, India [revised to Cuttack, Anaicut on the River Mahanadi]. Etymology: the name Rita is derived from the local name of the fish; the specific epithet comes from the Greek chrysos, meaning gold, in reference to the colour.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1114",
      "html": "<p>Asia: Mahanadi river system in Orissa and Madhya Pradesh, India. IUCN Red List category: Least Concern (last assessed 2010).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1114",
      "html": "<p>pH 6.0–7.0. Temperature 22–27°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1114",
      "html": "<p>Provide plenty of driftwood as hiding places.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1114",
      "html": "<p>Readily eats all manner of live and prepared foods. Will eat smaller fishes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1114",
      "html": "<p>Although a fairly peaceful fish, it will eat smaller tankmates and is therefore best kept only with large, robust fishes. Larger cyprinids (e.g. Osteochilus) and larger spiny eels (Mastacembelus) are ideal tankmates.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1114",
      "html": "<p>Males have an elongate genital papilla in front of the anal fin.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1114",
      "html": "<p>Not reported in the aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1114",
      "html": "<p>Bagrid catfishes of the genus Rita Bleeker, 1854 are found in large rivers throughout the Indian subcontinent and Myanmar, and can reach 1500 mm TL, although mature specimens of about 200–300 mm SL are more commonly encountered. The genus is diagnosed by a single pair of mandibular barbels, an elongated Weberian apparatus firmly sutured to the basioccipital, and the sensory canal on the posttemporal enclosed within the bone.</p><p>Eye large (30–40% HL), palatal teeth in a large quadrangular patch covering most of the palate, dorsal spine serrated.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1114",
      "html": "<p>The fishes of India Part 3, pp 455, Pl. 104 (fig. 1).</p>"
    }
  ],
